WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:33.633
[Narrator] During the last year, in the United States, there were approximately 10,000 open-heart operations performed

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:40.333
in which one or more heart valves were removed and replaced with an artificial device.

00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:53.199
And yet, the era of prosthetic heart valves began less than 20 years ago, in 1954, when Dr. Charles Hufnagel of Georgetown University

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:59.533
introduced a mechanical device which partially relieved aortic regurgitation.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:05.020
This prosthesis consisted of a plastic ball...

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:17.700
enclosed within a lucite cage which was designed to be rapidly placed in the descending thoracic aorta.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.900
External fixation rings held this prosthesis in place.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:34.300
Although the plastic valve was extremely noisy, thrombogenic, and relieved regurgitation from only the lower half of the body,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:41.620
it was of benefit to many patients and represented the first attempts at repairing

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.100
cardiac valvular dysfunction with a mechanical device.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:55.333
In the late 1950s, several surgeons including Hufnagel, Henry Bronson and Dwight Magoon

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:01.120
began replacing single damaged cusps of the aortic valve.

00:02:01.366 --> 00:02:10.820
In 1958, in a two-stage procedure, Dr. William H Muller of the University of Virginia first totally removed

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:14.460
an aortic valve and separately replaced each cusp.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:24.040
Early in 1959, Dr. Muller performed the first successful operation in which a damaged heart valve

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.700
was completely removed and replaced with an artificial device.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:36.820
This tricuspid Teflon prosthetic heart valve was used for the next several years by many cardiac surgeons.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:49.540
A later modification by Doctors Muller and James Littlefield, added more circumferential support to this Teflon prosthesis.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:57.560
Hemodynamically, this was a very acceptable alternate to the natural aortic valve.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:05.120
It was not obstructive, was totally free of embolic difficulties, and it was completely silent.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:13.520
Unfortunately, after 12 to 24 months, these Teflon leaflets stiffened and cracked,

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:21.566
resulting in recurrent aortic stenosis and regurgitation, and most of these leaflet valves were eventually

00:03:21.566 --> 00:03:27.040
replaced with later models of artificial heart valves.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:38.333
The first complete replacement of the mitral valve was performed at the National Heart Institute in 1960 by Drs. Andrew G Morrow,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.220
and Nina Braunwald.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:50.180
They employed an artificial heart valve constructed of polyurethane foam reinforced with background fabric.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:57.200
The leaflets were controlled by artificial chordae tendineae which were brought through

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:02.920
the left ventricular free wall and then adjusted to proper length and tension.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:20.880
Later, Dr. [?] and Dr. Morrow developed a bicuspid prosthesis made of Teflon fabric that was successfully implanted in South Africa.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:31.040
These fabric mitral valves suffered the same fate as those used in the aortic position.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:44.420
It was becoming obvious that any artificial heart valve using flexible components was entirely

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:54.860
dependent upon the flex life of the material used, and so attention was redirected to the use of rigid materials.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:05:03.300
Early in 1960, both Dr. Dwight Hartman of the Peter Bent Brigham Hospital in Boston,

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:14.260
and Dr. Albert Starr of the University of Oregon, were developing artificial heart valves utilizing the caged ball principal.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:23.700
Dr. Starr successfully implanted such a prosthesis in September of 1960.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:31.320
Since that time, rigid materials have been used in the manufacture of prosthetic heart valves.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:42.200
The only exception to this was a Teflon-coated silicone tricuspid prosthetic valve

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:47.260
which was developed by Dr. Hufnagel in the late 1960s.

00:05:47.266 --> 00:05:55.899
This valve underwent extensive and thoroughly rapid leaflet damage with large depositions

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:02.000
of plambus above the leaflets, and its use was quickly discontinued.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:15.340
In 1962, Dr. Harkin outlined the basic design criteria for the optimal prosthetic heart valve.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:29.540
It should have lasting physical and geometric features, and it should be capable of permanent fixation in the normal anatomic site.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:40.360
It should be chemically inert, non-thrombogenic, harmless to blood elements, and it must not annoy the patient.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:45.360
Patient annoyance can refer to many things:

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:58.640
excessive noise generated when two rigid materials strike each other, and such factors as the nuisance of lifelong anticoagulation.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:11.880
It must open and close promptly during the appropriate phase of the cardiac cycle, and it should offer no resistance to physiologic flows.

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:23.620
Since 1962, at the Clinic of Surgery of the National Heart and Lung Institute, the vast majority of prosthetic heart valves

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:33.320
placed in the aortic or mitral position, have been of the Starr-Edwards caged ball variety.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:42.820
Before discussing the evolution of the design of the Starr-Edwards valve, a discussion of its anatomy is necessary.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.180
At first glance, the mechanics of this valve seem simple enough.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:58.480
But it must be remembered that there are three distinct orifices which must be considered when implanting this type of prosthesis.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:11.660
The primary orifice represents the area of the inflow orifice at the seat of the valve.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:21.760
The secondary orifice is encountered when the ball is fully opened and represents the area,

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:27.800
a truncated cone, between the ball and the valve seat.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:42.940
The tertiary orifice represents the area between the diameter of the ball and the surrounding tissues, be it aorta or ventricular cavity.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:53.540
The next two photographs show examples of aortic valves in which tertiary orifice obstruction was present.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:58.420
Both are viewed from above through sectioned aorta.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:06.280
The area between the ball in the open position, and the adjoining aortic wall, is too small,

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:12.360
and a significant obstruction to forward flow has occurred.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:29.260
The first Starr-Edwards valves, Series 1000 for the aortic position, and 6000 for the mitral position, were implanted in 1961.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:42.460
The aortic model is recognizable by the silastic ball and the three metal studs that project from the valve seat.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:49.580
This early aortic valve had a thromboembolism rate of about 20 percent.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:10:00.780
The mitral prosthesis, the 6000 series, is recognizable by its heavy metal base and thick struts.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:09.060
This valve had an incidence of thromboembolic complications which exceeded 50 percent.

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:18.720
The greatest complication of these early Starr-Edwards valves was in the silastic ball.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:31.620
This silastic underwent lipid infiltration which led to changes in the shape of the ball, both shrinkage with ball escape,

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:42.733
and swelling with cracking of the ball which led to improper movement of the ball in the cage,

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:48.160
and a predisposition to thromboembolism formation.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:54.880
In 1966, the 1000 and 6000 series were discontinued.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:06.720
The new aortic models, 1200 and then 1260, featured new techniques of curing the silastic ball

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:11.160
and extended the cloth to cover the inflow orifice.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:34.320
The 1260 aortic model and the 6120 micro prosthesis, which has a greatly reduced metal base, continue in thoroughly widespread use.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:43.300
These models have a greatly lowered incidence of thromboembolism and are very quiet.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:50.900
At the National Heart and Lung Institute, we are personally reluctant to use any silastic ball,

00:11:52.800 --> 00:12:00.160
and we are apprehensive that the newer curing techniques will not eliminate, but only delay

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:09.200
lipid and fatty acid infiltration and eventual deterioration of the silastic.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:15.940
In 1967, there was a major modification of the Starr-Edwards prosthesis.

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:27.100
In an effort to reduce the incidence of thromboembolism, a completely cloth covered cage was introduced...

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:42.800
which utilized a hollow metal ball of stellite 21 which it has the same specific gravity as blood.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:58.940
The cloth covered models, series 2300 for aortic implantation and 6300 for the mitral position, had several difficulties.

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:07.960
Tissue ingrowth occurred about the primary orifice, thus decreasing the area

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:12.920
and establishing unacceptable gradients across the valve.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:25.640
In actuality, this tissue ingrowth at the valve seat can pile up and cause both primary and secondary orifice obstruction.

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:35.380
Tears and fragmentation of the single layer of cloth cover also occurred with some frequency.

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:49.780
The 2300 and 6300 series were modified to the 2310 and 6310 series respectively.

00:13:51.580 --> 00:14:02.760
This series incorporated a composite sheet of metal studs projecting through the cloth upon which the ball seats.

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:10.240
These seem to have illuminated the problem of tissue ingrowth at the primary orifice.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:20.120
This series also introduced a two-ply cloth cover which has decreased cloth wear.

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:27.640
In certain of the 2310 series, a disastrous complication occurred.

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:39.360
A close clearance model was introduced in 1970 in which the transverse distance between the pocket

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:42.300
and the cage was reduced.

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:53.960
These valves impacted in the open position with the acute onset of massive aortic regurgitation and sudden death.

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:04.900
This complication was also seen in the 6310 series of mitral valves.

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:20.460
This flaw was quickly eliminated and the present 2320 aortic and 6320 mitral prostheses have evolved.

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:26.980
These models are currently in use in our clinic.

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:38.666
They feature a metal [?], the composite seat, a wide distance between cage

00:15:38.666 --> 00:15:47.000
and pop-up, which flares even more near the apex, and a two-ply cloth cover.

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:59.540
Concern about the two-ply cloth undergoing wear and fragmentation has led Edwards Laboratory to introduce a new series,

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:13.820
2400 and 6400, which feature a thin inner metal track which is the only part of the covered cage which contacts the ball,

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.780
thus preventing damage to the cloth cover.

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:27.400
This model is in a developmental stage, and its major drawback is an excessive amount of noise.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:39.140
Many other cage-ball type artificial heart valves have been and are currently in use in the United States.

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:47.180
One of the most unique of these is the Magovern suture-less prosthesis.

00:16:47.640 --> 00:17:01.500
In this valve, sharp metal spikes contained in the seat are circumferentially impacted into the aortic annulus to keep the valve in position.

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:12.860
This has the advantage of a shorter operating time, but the disadvantage of a high incidence of para-basilar leaks

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:21.000
in the hands of the surgeon who is not thoroughly experienced in proper implantation techniques.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:25.280
It also uses a silastic ball.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:34.960
One instance for which the Magovern prosthesis is particularly suited is when the aortic annulus is extremely pliable,

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:42.220
secondary to either infection or extensive calcific or [?] degeneration.

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:52.740
In this situation, the Magovern prosthesis can be wedged into position and secured with good chances of remaining in place.

00:17:55.560 --> 00:18:04.120
The Smeloff Cutter caged ball prosthesis is a double cage of titanium with a silastic ball.

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:15.800
This design allows a smaller ball to be used than is possible in the Starr-Edwards model, since the ball does not seat on the rings,

00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:20.820
but passes through it to seat on the cage underneath.

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:34.900
The Debakey caged ball prosthesis made by Surgitool features a pyrolite carbon ball.

00:18:49.620 --> 00:19:01.440
The present Harken prosthesis has a cross-covered cage, a metal ball, and a plastic cover about the seat.

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:15.140
Another caged ball prosthesis which is in widespread use is the Braunwald-Cutter valve.

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:25.640
The concept of a cloth cover for a caged ball prosthesis was developed by doctors Braunwald and Morrow in this clinic.

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:36.400
The rationale was to eliminate thromboembolic complications, and was suggested to them by observation of a

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:41.460
thin ingrowth of tissue which occurred in the early Muller valves.

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:52.180
Dr. Braunwald's investigative work, which demonstrated the efficacy of the cloth cover in reducing thromboembolism,

00:19:52.700 --> 00:20:01.320
led both Cutter and Edwards Laboratories to use cloth coverings in their caged ball prosthesis.

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:09.800
The Braunwald-Cutter valve features a complete thin fabric cover,

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:26.260
which permits an ingrowth of a very thin layer of autologous tissue which does not compromise the diameter of the inflow orifice.

00:20:27.160 --> 00:20:29.800
A silicone ball is used.

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:45.720
When the ventricular cavity is large, the caged ball prosthesis is a satisfactory valve.

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:58.280
However, when the left ventricular cavity is small, as it usually is in pure mitral stenosis, the caged ball is often not acceptable.

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:07.600
Not only can the ball obstruct flow out of the left ventricle, as pointed out in the middle diagram,

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:17.460
but if the ball is in contact with ventricular muscle, inflow to the left ventricular cavity can be obstructed.

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:30.280
It is also possible for a mitral prosthesis, if it is large and placed too high, to impede closing of an aortic prosthesis

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:34.040
in a double-valve replacement.

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:45.160
However, the most common problem with a caged ball mitral valve is simply that the ventricular cavity is too small to contain it,

00:21:46.340 --> 00:21:49.860
and a form of tertiary orifice obstruction occurs.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:22:00.200
In an effort to solve this problem of size, the disc-type valvular prosthesis was developed.

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:12.780
This valve uses a lens-shaped topper which occupies a much smaller portion of the ventricular cavity.

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:24.360
Early prototypes of these valves were developed by Dr. Jerome Kay of the University of Southern California in Los Angeles,

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:37.720
Doctors Cross and Jones, and Doctors Earle Kay and Suzuki, all of Cleveland.

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:51.080
Dr. Dwight Harken was also a pioneer in the development of disc valves, as was Dr. [?] Seidel of Paris.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:59.620
There is only a small pressure gradient between the atrium and ventricles during diastole,

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:06.880
and thrombosis has been a great difficulty in these disc valves.

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:17.860
Efforts to decrease the amount of exposed metal as seen in the recent Kay-Shiley valve and the recent Harken valve,

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:24.340
have decreased but not eliminated the problem of thrombosis.

00:23:31.960 --> 00:23:42.680
Another difficulty with disc valves is muscle ingrowth from the left ventricle wall, which prevents normal function of the disc.

00:23:43.100 --> 00:23:48.480
Loose [?] have also obstructed the low-profile valves.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:24:00.860
In an effort to prevent muscle from the left ventricular free wall from obstructing the disc, a recent modification of the Kay-Shiley valve

00:24:01.960 --> 00:24:07.940
has a muscle guard which protects the disc from this type of obstruction.

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:19.380
Edwards Laboratory has also made a disc valve.

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:31.340
The early model, series 6500, used a stellite metal disc which had a very high incidence of thromboembolism,

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:37.060
and this has been modified through the 6520 series.

00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:52.780
Thromboembolism with this prosthesis has been reduced, but not eliminated.

00:24:55.140 --> 00:25:04.180
The Cooley-Bloodwell prosthesis has also been discontinued because of an unacceptable incidence of thromboembolism.

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:16.960
A very popular mitral valve prosthesis, which is currently in widespread use, is the bell valve manufactured by Surgitools.

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:22.860
This prosthesis features a Dacron velour covering.

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:31.460
Initial experience shows a very low incidence of thromboembolism with this prosthesis.

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:49.200
Recently, the Cooley-Cutter disk prosthesis has been introduced.

00:25:52.360 --> 00:26:02.600
In this valve, the disc is smaller than the internal orifice of the prosthesis and sits on the upstream retaining struts.

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:13.340
This principle allows small amounts of controlled regurgitation, which washes the disc and minimizes

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:17.140
the chances of disc impingement.

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:26.620
The retaining struts have no cross-members, which eliminates a focus for thrombosis.

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:36.880
This illustration demonstrates the potential for improved flow in the Cooley-Cutter prosthesis.

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:47.680
One can see the symmetrical but restricted flow that occurs when the disc is larger than the primary orifice.

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:58.540
On the bottom is seen the increase in central flow that is afforded by use of the smaller disc.

00:27:00.260 --> 00:27:12.840
Regardless of the low-profile principle of disc valves, tertiary orifice obstruction due to small size of the ventricular cavity can still occur.

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:23.900
One other type of artificial heart valve employs a disc on a pivot which gives unobstructed central flow.

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:33.060
An early model of this principle was used by Van Der Spuy.

00:27:34.640 --> 00:27:44.833
In this cloth covered toilet seat valve, the spring pivot point above the valve represented an area

00:27:44.840 --> 00:27:51.800
of stagnant flow and offered great potential for thrombus formation.

00:27:52.800 --> 00:28:06.440
The tilting disc valves which are popular today, all have projecting pivot points with [?] openings and areas of turbulence and stasis.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:14.920
The Waddel valve has recently been taken off the market because of the high incidence of thromboembolism.

00:28:16.900 --> 00:28:24.020
The disc in this valve is fixed and thrombosis occurred at these fixed points.

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:38.300
We are impressed with the Bjork-Shiley prosthesis which employs

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:44.200
the same basic principle, but in which the [?] disc is not fixed.

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:52.400
Hence, it can rotate and is in that respect, self-cleaning.

00:28:56.500 --> 00:29:08.340
The Bjork–Shiley prosthesis is our choice in aortic valve replacement in children, or when the annulus or aortic root are small.

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:13.960
It is also quite acceptable for use within an arterial graft.

00:29:15.940 --> 00:29:23.260
Besides artificial materials, there has been a large amount of time directed at the use of

00:29:23.260 --> 00:29:28.000
biological tissues in the manufacture of prosthetic heart valves.

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:33.533
Biological valves have been made of fascia lata and pericardium,

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:39.580
and heart valves from human cadavers and animals have been employed.

00:29:40.620 --> 00:29:49.440
In most cases, late fibrosis and calcification, with recurrent stenosis and regurgitation, have been the rule.

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:58.520
An exception to this has been the Reese-Hancock porcine xenograft.

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:08.800
This valve, which was designed by Dr. Robert Reese here at the National Heart and Lung Institute, for the mitral or

00:30:08.800 --> 00:30:20.580
tricuspid position, uses a pig aortic valve which is fixed and sterilized with glutaraldehyde and mounted on a flexible stent.

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:39.080
The valve has a low gradient, cannot be obstructed by the left ventricle in diastole and requires no anticoagulation.

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:46.280
Our experience with this prosthesis is into the fourth year,

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:54.400
and to date there have been no instances of thromboembolism or other sign of valve failure.

00:30:56.080 --> 00:31:01.500
Here is a valve which was removed at eight months because of a parabasilar leak,

00:31:02.680 --> 00:31:08.340
which demonstrates no sign of deterioration of the tissue leaflets.

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:22.660
These, then, represent most of the attempts at artificial construction of heart valves.

00:31:23.780 --> 00:31:30.840
It is obvious that none of them can compare with our natural valves, nor have Dr. Harken's criteria,

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:38.000
laid down in 1962, been met.

00:31:39.800 --> 00:31:46.680
Progress in technical developments and modifications of these valves will continue,

00:31:47.080 --> 00:31:57.060
and if the next 15 years are as fruitful as the last, then we shall see many more exciting developments in artificial heart valves.

00:32:00.260 --> 00:32:10.240
After this motion picture was completed, a historical inaccuracy and a significant omission were discovered by the authors.

00:32:11.900 --> 00:32:19.760
In September of 1958, three months prior to the first aortic valve replacement by Dr. Muller,

00:32:19.760 --> 00:32:29.660
Dr. C. Walton Lillehei of the University of Minnesota successfully replaced a cardiac valve in the normal anatomic position.

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:41.033
Following an unsuccessful commissurotomy and [?], an aortic valve was excised and a silastic flap placed

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:44.760
in the subcoronary position as depicted above.

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:49.240
The patient survived for at least 23 months.

00:32:50.100 --> 00:32:56.360
One other such procedure was successfully performed by Dr. Lillehei the following year.

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:08.480
We also failed to mention the silastic butterfly leaflet prosthetic heart valve designed by Doctors Vincent Gott

00:33:08.960 --> 00:33:12.780
and Ronald Daggett of the University of Wisconsin.

00:33:13.480 --> 00:33:22.980
These valves were implanted clinically from 1963 to 1965 in both the aortic and mitral position.

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:30.760
A number of these valves that were inserted eight to ten years ago are still functioning well.

00:33:32.140 --> 00:33:37.820
Their use was discontinued because of an unacceptable incidence of thrombosis

00:33:38.240 --> 00:33:44.600
that occurred on the down stream side of the leaflet at the area of the hinged site.

